## Lab 9: Build a Very Simple Quality Gate

Create a basic classroom check:

```bash
cat > ~/quality-lab/simple-quality-gate.sh <<'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

PROJECT_DIR="${1:-.}"
FAILED=0

echo "Checking project: $PROJECT_DIR"
echo

if grep -RniE \
'password[[:space:]]*=|secret[[:space:]]*=|api[_-]?key[[:space:]]*=' \
"$PROJECT_DIR"; then
    echo
    echo "FAILED: Possible hard-coded secret found."
    FAILED=1
else
    echo "PASSED: No obvious hard-coded secret found."
fi

if grep -RniE 'TODO|FIXME|HACK' "$PROJECT_DIR"; then
    echo
    echo "WARNING: Unfinished-work markers found."
fi

echo

if [ "$FAILED" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "QUALITY GATE: PASSED"
    exit 0
else
    echo "QUALITY GATE: FAILED"
    exit 1
fi
EOF
```

Make it executable:

```bash
chmod +x ~/quality-lab/simple-quality-gate.sh
```

Run it:

```bash
~/quality-lab/simple-quality-gate.sh ~/quality-lab
```

Display the exit status:

```bash
echo $?
```

The conventional result is:

```text
0 = Successful or passed
Non-zero = Failed
```

### Teaching Point

A CI/CD pipeline can use an exit status to decide whether to continue or stop.

This classroom script is extremely simple, but it demonstrates the same decision pattern:

```text
Checks run
    |
    v
Conditions evaluated
    |
    v
PASS or FAIL
    |
    +---- Exit 0     → Continue
    |
    +---- Exit 1     → Stop
```

